[{"name":"mcp__umbraco__create_media","description":"Upload any media file to Umbraco (images, documents, audio, video, SVG, or custom types).

  Pick the sourceType that matches where the file lives:
  - If the file is attached to this chat or you just generated it → sourceType="file" (the host fills in the file reference automatically).
  - If you have a public direct-download URL → sourceType="url".
  - For tiny inline payloads (≤10 KiB decoded) → sourceType="base64".

  Media Types:
  - Image: jpg, png, gif, webp, etc. (supports cropping)
  - Article: pdf, docx, doc (documents)
  - Audio: mp3, wav, etc.
  - Video: mp4, webm, etc.
  - SVG: svg files only
  - File: any other file type
  - Custom: any custom media type created in Umbraco

  Source Types:
  1. filePath - Most efficient for local files, works with any size
     SECURITY: Requires UMBRACO_ALLOWED_MEDIA_PATHS environment variable
     to be configured with comma-separated allowed directories.
     Example: UMBRACO_ALLOWED_MEDIA_PATHS="/tmp/uploads,/var/media"
  2. url - Stream from any public direct-download URL (Drive / Dropbox / etc.). Use this for anything above 10 KiB that isn't already attached to the chat.
  3. file - Stream a host-attached file. The connector injects { download_url, file_id, mime_type, file_name } on the 'file' field automatically when the user attached a file or you generated one in this chat — prefer this over url for chat-bound files.
  4. base64 - ONLY for tiny inline payloads; server hard-rejects decoded base64 over 10 KiB to prevent LLM-truncated corrupt files.

  The tool automatically:
  - Creates temporary files
  - Detects and validates media types (auto-corrects SVG vs Image)
  - Configures correct property editors (ImageCropper vs UploadField)
  - Cleans up temporary files

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__umbraco__create_media(args: {
  // [raw] Host-injected file object (required if sourceType is 'file'). ChatGPT's connector populates this automatically when the user attached a file or you generated one in this chat — leave it for the host to fill, do not synthesise it yourself.
  file?: {
  // Temporary URL the host provides to fetch the file bytes
  download_url: string;
  // Persistent file identifier from the host
  file_id: string;
  // Original file name if the host knows it
  file_name?: string;
  // MIME type if the host knows it
  mime_type?: string;
};
  // Base64-encoded file data (required if sourceType is 'base64'). HARD LIMIT: decoded payload must be ≤10 KiB; the server rejects anything larger because LLMs reliably truncate big base64 strings or substitute thumbnail previews, both producing corrupt files. Use sourceType='url' or 'file' for everything bigger.
  fileAsBase64?: string;
  // Absolute path to the file (required if sourceType is 'filePath')
  filePath?: string;
  // [raw] Public, direct-download URL to fetch the file from (required if sourceType is 'url'). Must be reachable without authentication. Share/viewer links (e.g. drive.google.com/file/d/<id>/view, Dropbox ?dl=0, OneDrive view URLs) must be converted to their direct-download equivalent first — Google Drive: drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=<id>. Uploads are streamed, so multi-MB files round-trip without timing out.
  fileUrl?: string;
  // Media type: 'Image', 'Article', 'Audio', 'Video', 'SVG', 'File', or custom media type name
  mediaTypeName: string;
  // The name of the media item
  name: string;
  // Parent folder ID (defaults to root)
  parentId?: string;
  // Media source type: 'filePath' for local files (Node stdio only), 'url' for public direct-download URLs (streamed; preferred for everything not already attached to the chat), 'file' for host-injected attachments — the connector populates the 'file' object automatically when the user attached a file or you generated one in this chat, 'base64' for TINY inline payloads only — the server rejects base64 above 10 KiB decoded to stop LLM-truncated or thumbnail-preview base64 from persisting as corrupt files
  sourceType: "filePath" | "url" | "file" | "base64";
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{ id: string; message: string; name: string; }>>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__umbraco__create_media_folder","description":"Creates a new folder in the media library. Use this to organize media items into folders. For uploading actual media files (images, documents, etc.), use the create-media tool instead.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__umbraco__create_media_folder(args: {
  // The name of the folder
  name: string;
  // Parent folder ID. If not provided, the folder will be created at the root of the media library.
  parentId?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{ id: string; message: string; name: string; }>>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__umbraco__create_media_multiple","description":"Batch upload multiple media files to Umbraco (maximum 20 files per batch).

  Use this — not several separate create-media calls — when the user attached or generated multiple files in this chat (sourceType="file"), or has a list of public URLs to upload (sourceType="url"). ChatGPT's connector populates each entry's 'file' field automatically.

  Source Types:
  1. filePath - Most efficient for local files, works with any size
     SECURITY: Requires UMBRACO_ALLOWED_MEDIA_PATHS environment variable
     to be configured with comma-separated allowed directories.
     Example: UMBRACO_ALLOWED_MEDIA_PATHS="/tmp/uploads,/var/media"
  2. url - Stream from any public direct-download URL (Drive / Dropbox / etc.).
  3. file - Stream a host-attached file. The connector injects { download_url, file_id, ... } per entry automatically when the user attached or generated files in this chat — prefer this over 'url' for chat-bound files.

  Note: base64 is not supported for batch uploads due to token usage.

  The tool processes up to 4 uploads in parallel and returns detailed results for each file in input order. If some files fail, others continue processing (continue-on-error strategy).

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__umbraco__create_media_multiple(args: {
  // Array of files to upload (maximum 20 files per batch)
  files: Array<{
  // [raw] Host-injected file reference (required if sourceType is 'file'). ChatGPT's connector populates this per entry automatically when files are attached.
  file?: {
  // Temporary URL the host provides to fetch the file bytes
  download_url: string;
  // Persistent file identifier from the host
  file_id: string;
  // Original file name if the host knows it
  file_name?: string;
  // MIME type if the host knows it
  mime_type?: string;
};
  // Absolute path to the file (required if sourceType is 'filePath')
  filePath?: string;
  // [raw] Public direct-download URL (required if sourceType is 'url'). Drive / Dropbox share links must be converted to their direct-download equivalent first — see the create-media tool description for the format. Streamed, so multi-MB files round-trip without timing out.
  fileUrl?: string;
  // Optional override: 'Image', 'Article', 'Audio', 'Video', 'SVG', 'File', or custom media type name. If not specified, defaults to 'File'
  mediaTypeName?: string;
  // The name of the media item
  name: string;
}>;
  // Parent folder ID (defaults to root)
  parentId?: string;
  // Media source type: 'filePath' for local files (most efficient, Node stdio only), 'url' for public direct-download URLs (streamed), 'file' for host-injected attachments — the connector populates each entry's 'file' object automatically when the user attached or generated files in this chat. Base64 is not supported for batch uploads due to token usage — use single-file create-media for tiny inline payloads.
  sourceType: "filePath" | "url" | "file";
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{ results: Array<{ error?: string; id?: string; name: string; success: boolean; }>; summary: string; }>>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__umbraco__create_template","description":"Creates a new template

